course: dictionary
lesson: 13985
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The clues around the word tell you which meaning the writer wants. «The logger cut down the tall pine tree.» The clues cut down and tree show that a logger is a person who cuts down trees.
explain: «The data logger measured the temperature every five seconds.» Here the clues are data and measured, so a logger is a device that records numbers over time. Ask yourself which meaning fits the sentence, then try it in place of the word to check.
explain: «The logger showed that the computer crashed at three o'clock.» A log is a list of what happened, so a logger on a computer writes that list. One word, three meanings: a lumberjack, a recording device, or a computer program. Context tells you which one.
fact: A logger is a person whose job is cutting down trees and taking the logs away. Another word for this is lumberjack.
fact: A data logger is a device that records information again and again over time, such as temperature, light or sound.
fact: On a computer, a logger is a small program that writes a list, called a log, of what happens.
fact: Loggers work in forests in places such as Canada, the USA, Australia and New Zealand.
fact: «Illegal loggers» are people who cut down trees against the law, and this is a topic people debate, especially when rainforests are cut down.
exercise: «The logger cut down the tall pine before lunch.» What is a logger in this sentence? | A computer program that writes a list | *A person who cuts down trees | A device that records temperature | A heavy wooden plank
exercise: «The data logger recorded the temperature every ten seconds.» What is a data logger? | A tall tree in the forest | A box for keeping lunch cold | A person who writes poems | *A device that records information over time
exercise: «The logger showed that the computer crashed at three o'clock.» What does logger mean here? | A camp in the woods | A lumberjack | A heavy wooden log | *A program that writes a list of what happens
test: «Illegal loggers cut down trees in the rainforest without permission.» What do illegal loggers mean? | Children who play in the woods | Doctors who look after sick trees | Workers who plant new forests | *People who cut down trees against the law
test: «The data logger recorded the sound in the classroom every minute.» What does the sentence tell us the data logger does? | It turns the lights on and off | *It records sound again and again over time | It writes a poem about the classroom | It cuts down trees in the forest
exam: Mia sees the word logger in three different texts. Why does the context matter? | Every use of the word means exactly the same thing | The dictionary always gives the first meaning, so you never need context | Context only matters in stories, not in science or history | *The clues around the word show which meaning the writer means
